What should we empathize with next?

I’d deliberately choose things that violate a different human assumption each time:

* Octopus — distributed neural control; what changes when substantial processing occurs throughout the arms?
* Bat — construct spatial experience primarily through returning sound; world as delay and echo.
* Electric fish — experience nearby objects through perturbations in a self-generated electric field; sense by emitting and reading what comes back. That’s almost Semantic Dye made biological.
* Spider/web — externalize sensing into a tensioned structure; the web becomes an extended sensory surface. Very Pantheon.
* Plant/root system — growth as exploration, distributed chemical/environmental sensing, no locomotion in the animal sense.
* Migratory bird — navigation with sensory cues humans don’t consciously possess in the same way; add huge spatial scale to “home.”
* Immune system — self/non-self discrimination, distributed memory, escalation, tolerance, false positives. Particularly useful for governance.
* Embryo — no finished blueprint being assembled piece-by-piece; local developmental interactions progressively generate differentiated structure.
* Ecosystem — deliberately destroy the assumption that the empathized-with entity has one body or one objective.
* Language — the provocative one. Pretend you are a language competing for continued reproduction across brains, books, machines, and centuries.
* A city — roads as circulation, buildings as durable memory, people as rapidly changing components, laws as regulatory machinery.
* A forest fire — empathize with a process rather than an organism: fuel, boundary, propagation, wind, extinction. Useful precisely because we mustn’t sneak intention into it.
* A river — no central planner; constraints plus landscape continuously produce path. Then give the river human foresight and ask what watershed engineering it discovers.
* A dying star — radically different scale and timescale. What does “state transition” mean when your meaningful unit is millions of years?

The strongest Survey Corps expeditions should choose targets whose affordance spaces are maximally unlike ours.

Similarity gives empathy.

Difference gives discovery.

